.Sh DESCRIPTION
The lyparseJSON library is an implementation of a JSON parser built on code
generated by
.Xr lex 1
and
.Xr yacc 1
.Pp
The library can be used to parse JSON to an internal representation which can
be queried, and to produce JSON from internal representation. The internal
representation of JSON is intentionally hidden from the user of the library, to
prevent corruption of data.
.Pp
Library calls can be split into the following groups:
.Pp
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
Functions to reading JSON (converting to internal representation)
.It
Functions to write JSON (converting from internal representation)
.It
Functions to extract values from JSON
.It
Functions to build JSON from values
.It
Functions to debug and get error information
.El
.Ss Functions to read JSON
There are two ways to read JSON, from a file or from a buffer.
.Pp
The function to
read JSON from a file is
.Fn parse_JSON_from_file
, and takes a single argument
.Fa JSONfile
, which is a FILE pointer, obtained from calling
.Xr fopen 3
or similar function.
.Pp
To read JSON from a buffer, use the function
.Fn parse_JSON_from_buffer
, which takes three arguments. The first,
.Fa JSONdata
, is a point to a buffer that contains the JSON data. The second,
.Fa JSONdatalen
, is the length of the data in the buffer. The buffer itself does not need to
be NULL terminated. The third argument,
.Fa makecopy
, tells the library whether or not to make a copy of the buffer before parsing
it. If the buffer is greater in length that the data by two or more characters
you can choose to parse the JSON directly in the buffer by setting the argument
to
.Em false
(the two character requirement is a function of the code produced by
lex and yacc). If set to
.Em true
, a copy of the buffer is made. Note that if JSON is parsed from the buffer
directly, the contents of the buffer will be modified. If you want to preserve
the JSON in the buffer, always set the third argument to
.Em true .
.Ss Functions to write JSON
There are two functions to write JSON. The first
.Fn write_JSON_to_file
is used to write JSON to a file. It takes two arguments. The first,
.Fa parsedJSON
, is the internal representation of JSON, and the second,
.Fa JSONfile
, is a FILE pointer opened using fopen () or similar. The function returns a
.Vt bool
value, which is
.Em true
if the write was successful and
.Em false
if it was not.
.Pp
The second function is
.Fn write_JSON_to_buffer
which takes two arguments. The first,
.Fa parsedJSON
, is the internal representation of JSON, and the second,
.Fa numbyteswritten
, is a pointer to an
.Vt integer
that contains the number of bytes written to the buffer. The function
calculates how much buffer space is necessary to write out JSON, and uses
.Xr malloc 3
to allocate the space. The buffer is returned when the function exits. If the
function fails to allocate memory for the buffer, or to write the JSON to the
buffer, the function returns NULL and the value of
.Fa numbyteswritten
is zero. It is the responsibility of the caller to free the buffer returned
when it is no longer required.
.Ss Functions to extract values from JSON
Values can be extracted from the in-memory representation of JSON using a
variety of funtions, with most designed to extract a specific value type.
.Pp
JSON is represented by an opaque type
.Vt JSON .
To determine what the JSON value type is, the function
.Fn get_JSON_value_type
can be used. It returns an enumerated type
.Dv JSON_VALUETYPE .
The values of this enumerated type corresponding to JSON values are:
.Pp
.Bl -inset -compact -offset 8
.It JSON_VALUE_STRING for character strings
.It JSON_VALUE_NUMBER for numbers (integers or doubles)
.It JSON_VALUE_OBJECT for objects
.It JSON_VALUE_ARRAY for arrays
.It JSON_VALUE_BOOLEAN for boolean values
.It JSON_VALUE_NULL for null values
.El
.Pp
If the enumerated value is
.Em JSON_VALUE_ARRAY
or
.Em JSON_VALUE_OBJECT
the library provides functions that allow you to iterate through the array
retrieving values from it, and retrieve member names and values from objects.
.Pp
To iterate through values in an array, use the functions
.Fn get_first_element_in_JSON_array
and
.Fn get_next_element_in_JSON_array .
Both functions take a single argument, which is the opaque data type
.Vt JSON
that refers to the array, and return a
.Vt JSON
data type that refers to the value in the array. The value NULL is returned
if the array is empty or if there are no more members in an array to retrieve.
Note that
.Fn get_first_element_in_JSON_array
must be called before calling
.Fn get_next_element_in_JSON_array
, otherwise the results can be unpredictable. To check if an array is empty,
the function
.Fn is_JSON_array_empty
can be called. It takes a data type of
.Vt JSON
which refers to the array, and returns a
.Vt bool
value. The value is
.Em true
if the array is empty, and
.Em false
if it is not.
.Pp
When the JSON value is an object, the functions
.Fn get_first_membername_in_JSON_object
and
.Fn get_next_membername_in_JSON_object
can be used to get the first and subsequent names of members of the object. As
with arrays, you must get the first member's name before attempting to get
subsequent names, otherwise the results are unpredictable. Both functions take
the
.Vt JSON
opaque data type that refers to the JSON object, and both return the member
name. When the object is empty, or there are no more members in the object, the
functions return
.Em NULL .
To get the value associated with a named member of the object, the function
.Fn get_membervalue_in_JSON_object
is used. It takes three arguments. The first argument is
.Fa parsedJSONobject
, which is the
.Vt JSON
value that refers to the object. The second,
.Fa membername
is the member name, passed as a string. The third argument is
.Fa convertunicode
, which is a
.Vt bool
data type and directive to the function whether or not to convert
.Xr utf8 5
characters in the
.Fa membername
argument to the format \\Uxxxx where x is a hexadecimal digit. If the value is
.Em true
then characters are converted. If
.Em false
characters are not. The function returns an opaque
.Vt JSON
data type to the value which can be queried with
.Fn get_JSON_value_type
to identify its JSON value type. If the member name specified in the second
argument does not exist in the object, the returned value is NULL. Note that
the desired member name may exist in the object but the function returns NULL,
due to the conversion (or lack of conversion) of the
.Fa membername
argument based on the
value of the
.Fa convertunicode
argument.
.Pp
To get a string value from JSON, use the function
.Fn get_JSON_string .
It takes a single argument, an opaque
.Vt JSON
data type that represents a string. The function returns a pointer to a string
in a buffer that is allocated to hold it. It is the caller's responsibility to
free the allocated buffer when it is no longer required. The function will
a NULL if the buffer cannot be allocated or some other internal error occurred. If the JSON
string is empty, an empty string in returned (not a NULL).
.Pp
To get a number value from JSON, one of three functions can be used. Numbers
are stored internally as strings until retrieved. To get the number as a string
use the function
.Fn get_JSON_number_string .
The function takes a single argument, the opaque data type
.Vt JSON
, and returns a character string. The function allocates buffer space for the
string. It is the caller's responsibility to delete the buffer when it is no
longer required. The function returns a NULL if the buffer cannot be allocated
or if some other internal error occurred. To retrieve the number value as a
.Vt long
or
.Vt double
data type, use
.Fn get_JSON_number_long
or
.Fn get_JSON_number_double
respectively. Both functions take a single argument, the opaque data type
.Vt JSON
and return the number value as the type specified. The function
.Fn get_JSON_number_type
can be used to determine whether the number is an integer or decimal number. It
takes the opaque data type
.Vt JSON
and returns an enumerated data type, which is one of:
.Pp
.Bl -inset -compact -offset 8
.It JSON_VALUE_NUMBER_INTEGER where the number is an integer
.It JSON_VALUE_NUMBER_DOUBLE where the number is a decimal or exponential value
.El
.Pp
To obtain a boolean value from JSON, use the function
.Fn get_JSON_boolean .
The function takes a single argument, the opaque data type
.Vt JSON
and returns a
.Vt bool
value (
.Em true
or
.Em false
). The function will return
.Em false
when an internal error occurs. See
.Sx ERRORS
below for details how to check whether an error occurred.
.Pp
There is no library function to obtain a null value from JSON. To determine if
a value is a null value, simply use the function
.Fn get_JSON_value_type
and check if the return value is the enumerated data value
.Em JSON_VALUE_NULL .
.Pp
.Ss Functions to build JSON from values
.Pp
The library stores JSON internally using the opaque data type
.Vt JSON .
The functions to build JSON from native data types all return a
.Vt JSON
data type. Various pieces of JSON can be combined into arrays and objects,
which themselves can be combined into other arrays and objects. If an error
occurs when creating a JSON value, functions will return a NULL pointer. See
.Sx ERRORS
below for more details.
.Pp
Arrays and objects can be created using the functions
.Fn create_JSON_array
and
.Fn create_JSON_object .
Both take no arguments, and return an empty array or object, to which other
JSON can be added.
.Pp
To add members to a JSON array, use the function
.Fn add_element_to_JSON_array .
The function takes two arguments. The first is
.Fa array
which is a
.Vt JSON
data type value returned from
.Fn create_JSON_array
, and the second is
.Fa value
which is the
.Vt JSON
data type value representing JSON created by any of the functions listed in this
section.
.Pp
To add members and their names to an object, use the function
.Fn add_member_to_JSON_object .
The function takes four arguments. The first is
.Fa object
, which is the
.Vt JSON
data type value created by the function
.Fn create_JSON_object .
The second is
.Fa name
, which is the member name of the value that will be added to the object. The
third argument is a
.Vt bool
data type 
.Fa convertunicode
, which the library uses to determine whether or not to convert Unicode found
in the
.Fa name
argument to the format \\Uxxxx. The last argument is
.Fa value
, which is a
.Vt JSON
data type of any JSON value (array, boolean, object, null, number, or string).
The return value is the object itself, or NULL if an error occurred.
.Pp
To create a JSON string value, use the function
.Fn create_JSON_string .
The function takes two arguments. The first is
.Fa string
, which is a pointer to a NULL-terminated character string. The second argument
is a
.Vt bool
data type named
.Fa convertunicode
, which instructs the library whether or not to convert any Unicode characters
to the format \\Uxxxx.
.Pp
There are three functions to create a JSON number. The first function,
.Fn create_JSON_number
takes a string representatiion of a number (an integer or a double) and simply
copies it to the internal representation. No validation is performed to ensure
that the string is actually a number. The second function
.Fn create_JSON_number_long
takes a
.Vt long
data type. The third function
.Fn create_JSON_number_double
takes a
.Vt double
data type.
.Pp
Boolean JSON values are created using the function
.Fn create_JSON_boolean .
The function takes a single argument of the
.Vt bool
data type, which is either
.Em true
or
.Em false.
.Pp
Null JSON values are created using the function
.Fn create_JSON_null
, which takes no arguments.
.Ss Functions to debug and get error information
The library can print out verbose debugging messages to
.Em stderr
if enabled. This is done using the function
.Fn set_parse_JSON_debug
which takes a single
.Vt bool
argument. If the argument is
.Em true
debugging is enabled, and if
.Em false
no debugging messages are written out. When debugging is enabled, debug
information from the lexical analyzer and parsing functions generated by lex
and yacc is also written out.
.Pp
The last error encountered by the library can be obtained using the function
.Fn get_last_JSON_error
, which takes no arguments.
The function returns a data type
.Vt JSONERROR
, which is an integer. A descriptive error string can be obtained using the
function
.Fn get_last_JSON_error_string .
This function takes no arguments. Note that unlike other functions in the
library that returns a string, this function does not allocate memory for the
error message returned, and it should not be modified nor freed by the caller.
.Pp
For more details on error handling, see the section
.Sx ERRORS
, below.
.\" The following requests should be uncommented and used where appropriate.
.\" This next request is for sections 2 and 3 function return values only.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Library functions except
.Fn free_parsed_JSON
and
.Fn set_parse_JSON_debug
all return values. In some cases a function will return a value even though an
error occurred. To check if an error occurred when a value is returned, use the
function
.Fn get_last_JSON_error .
If no error occurred, the return value of this function will be
.Em JSON_OK.
.Pp
For functions that return a string or the opaque data type
.Vt JSON
, a NULL value means that an error occurred. Details of the error can be
obtained using the functions
.Fn get_last_JSON_error
and
.Fn get_last_JSON_error_string .
For more details, see the section
.Sx ERRORS
, below.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The following examples show how to parse JSON and extract values from it, and
how to build JSON.
.Ss Parsing JSON
In the following example, JSON is parsed from a buffer, and then an array of
objects is iterated over, and members extracted. It assumes all the array
members are objects.
.Bd -literal -offset
char *rawjson, *member, *valstr;
int rawjsonlen;
double valnum;
bool valbool;
JSON array, object, value;

/* Call a function to load JSON into a buffer */
\&...
\&...
if ((array = parse_JSON_from_buffer (rawjson, rawjsonlen, true))
        == (JSON) 0) {
        /* An error occurred, so display an error and return */

        fprintf (stderr, "Error parsing JSON - %s (%s)\\n",
                get_last_JSON_error (),
                get_last_JSON_error_string ());
        return;
}

/* Go through the array, getting the members */

object = get_first_element_in_JSON_array (array);
while (object != (JSON) 0) {
        /* Process the object, enumerating its members */

        member = get_first_membername_in_JSON_object (object);
        while (member != (char *) 0) {
                /* Display the member name and then print the value... */

                printf ("Name: %s Value: %s", member);
                value = get_membervalue_in_JSON_object (
                        object, member, true);

                switch (get_JSON_value_type (value) {
                case JSON_VALUE_STRING:
                        valstr = get_JSON_string (value);
                        printf ("%s\\n", valstr);
                        free (valstr);
                        break;

                case JSON_VALUE_NUMBER:
                        valnum = get_JSON_number_double (value);
                        printf ("%e\\n", valnum);
                        break;

                case JSON_VALUE_BOOLEAN:
                        valbool = get_JSON_boolean (value);
                        printf ("%s\\n" (valbool ? "true" : "false"));
                        break;

                case JSON_VALUE_NULL:
                        printf ("null\\n");
                        break;

                case JSON_VALUE_ARRAY:
                case JSON_VALUE_OBJECT:
                        /* Call a function to handle these */

                        ...
                        ...
                        break;
                
                default:
                        /* Whoops, we got some unknown type */

                        fprintf (stderr, "Unknown JSON value type!\\n");
                        return false;
                        break;
                }

                /* Get next member */

                member = get_next_membername_in_JSON_object (object);
        }

        /* Get next object in JSON array of objects */

        object = get_next_element_in_JSON_array (array);
}

/* Free the parsed JSON */

free_parsed_JSON (array);
.Ed
.Pp
.Ss Building JSON
In the following example, an object is built and added to an array of objects
and then written to a buffer.
.Bd -literal -offset
JSON array, object, value;
char *string, *buffer;
int n, i, number, numbytes;
bool trueorfalse;

/* Create the array */

if ((array = create_JSON_array ()) == (JSON) 0) {
        /* An error occurred, and we could not create an array */

        fprintf (stderr, "Unable to create array - %d (%s)\\n",
                get_last_JSON_error (),
                get_last_JSON_error_string ());
        return;
}

/* Determine how many objects we need to add to the array */

i = ...;

for (n = 0; n < i; n ++) {
        /* Create the object we will populate and add to the array */

        if ((object = create_JSON_object ()) == (JSON) 0) {
                /* An error occurred */

                fprintf (stderr, "Unable to create object - %d (%s)\\n",
                        get_last_JSON_error (),
                        get_last_JSON_error_string ());
                return;
        }

        /* Get the string, number, and boolean values */

        string = ...;       /* Employee name */
        number = ...;       /* Employee Id */
        trueorfalse = ...;  /* Manager ? */

        /* Add the string, number, and boolean to the object */

        value = create_JSON_string (string, false);
        add_member_to_JSON_object (object, "Empname", false, value);
        value = create_JSON_number_long (number);
        add_member_to_JSON_object (object, "Empid", false, value);
        value = create_JSON_boolean (trueorfalse);
        add_member_to_JSON_object (object, "Ismanager", false, value);

        /* Add the object to the array */

        add_element_to_JSON_array (array, object);
}

/* Write the JSON to a buffer */

buffer = write_JSON_to_buffer (array, &numbytes);
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Pp
To get detailed diagnostic messages written to
.Em stderr
, use the function
.Fn set_parse_JSON_debug
and set the argument to
.Em true .
.Sh ERRORS
The library tracks errors internally, and makes the result of an operation
available to ther caller using the function
.Fn get_last_JSON_error .
The possible values returned are:
.Bl -column Value	Meaning
.It JSON_OK	No error
.It JSON_NOJSON	No JSON to work with (soft error)
.It JSON_BADJSON	Syntax error in JSON being parsed
.It JSON_NOTANARRAY	Value is not an array
.It JSON_NOTANOBJECT	Value if not an object
.It JSON_NOTANOBJECTMEMBER	Object member not found
.It JSON_INCORRECTVALUETYPE	Expecting a different type
.It JSON_INVALIDSTRING	The string was not valid
.It JSON_INTERNALERROR	Probable memory corruption
.It JSON_GENERALERROR	Error in C LIbrary (e.g. malloc)
.El
.Pp
A string that contains specific details of the last error encountered is
returned with the function
.Fn get_last_JSON_error_string .
.Pp
Any function that returns A
.Vt JSON
data type will return NULL if an error occurs. However, the functions to get
the first and next elements in an array and member names in an object will also
return NULL if the array is empty or all elements have been read from it, or
the object is empty or all members have been retrieved from it. For this reason 
you should use
.Fn get_last_JSON_error
when a NULL is returned to make sure that an error
did not occur.
.Sh STANDARDS
.Pp
The library implements a parser that conforms to Ecma International Standard
No. 404 (ECMA-404). It also implements the Internet Engineering Task Force
(IETF) Request For Comment (RFC) 8259 The JavaScript Object Notation (JSON)
Data Interchange Format.
.Pp
